WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
Is there enough ATP?

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.840
A healthy cell holds its ATP nearly constant, so ATP is a poor alarm.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.640
The kinase AMPK reads AMP instead.

4
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:24.170
Adenylate kinase keeps the three adenine nucleotides near equilibrium.

5
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.870
Two ADP make one ATP and one AMP.

6
00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:39.990
Divide by ATP. The AMP to ATP ratio goes as the square of the ADP to ATP ratio.

7
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:46.923
At rest, ATP is 5 millimolar and AMP is 0.025.

8
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.543
Let work use up a tenth of the ATP.

9
00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:59.743
ATP falls to 4.5, ADP settles near 0.93, and AMP becomes 0.096.

10
00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:05.183
ATP fell 10 percent, and AMP rose almost fourfold.

11
00:01:05.943 --> 00:01:09.023
The needle has barely moved, and the light is on.

12
00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:16.817
In AMPK, alpha carries the kinase, beta holds the complex together, and gamma

13
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:18.017
carries the sensor.

14
00:01:18.897 --> 00:01:25.977
AMP, ADP and ATP compete for sites on gamma, so gamma reads their ratio.

15
00:01:26.857 --> 00:01:32.177
The main switch is one threonine on alpha, phosphorylated by LKB1.

16
00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:38.557
With AMP on gamma, that phosphate is harder to remove, and by most accounts easier

17
00:01:38.557 --> 00:01:44.177
to add. AMP moves most, so AMPK reads AMP.
